    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Benign prostatic hyperplasia with severe lower urinary tract symptoms (N40.1)</content>. Prostate about 85 mL on ultrasound with a modest median lobe, benign on examination. PSA 4.9 ng/mL, drawn before catheterization, gives a PSA density of about 0.06, which fits benign enlargement; in keeping with his wishes and his age, no prostate MRI or biopsy is advised. Urology confirms bladder outlet obstruction from benign prostatic hyperplasia as the cause of the retention. [S]<list><item>Start finasteride 5 mg daily, as Dr. Marsh-Okoro advised; it shrinks the prostate over three to six months and halves the PSA, so later PSA values will be doubled for interpretation.</item><item>Continue tamsulosin 0.4 mg at bedtime.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic urinary retention, low risk once drained (R33.8)</content>. Creatinine 1.18 mg/dL (eGFR 63), down from 1.42, with urea nitrogen 18 mg/dL and normal electrolytes: the raised creatinine was from obstruction and has recovered. No hydronephrosis. Trabeculated bladder wall from long-standing obstruction. Retention is low risk now that it is drained. [S]<list><item>Keep the catheter for now; trial without catheter at about six weeks, after a longer course of tamsulosin, as urology advised.</item><item>Catheter care reviewed; change every four weeks by the clinic nurse.</item><item>He prefers the indwelling catheter to intermittent self-catheterization, which was offered.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Urinary retention, cause now established (R33.9)</content>. Superseded by the diagnosis of benign prostatic hyperplasia with chronic urinary retention. [S]<list><item>Managed under the two diagnoses above.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>IPSS symptom and quality-of-life scores</td><td>Baseline and every follow-up visit once voiding</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not scored while the catheter drains the bladder; baseline 26 with a quality-of-life score of 5</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Post-void residual by bladder scan</td><td>With retention, at each trial without catheter, and after it</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Catheter in place; baseline 640 mL</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Creatinine, eGFR, and electrolytes</td><td>With retention or suspected obstructive uropathy; after drainage and at each trial without catheter</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Creatinine 1.18 mg/dL, eGFR 63, urea nitrogen 18 mg/dL; electrolytes normal</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Kidney and bladder ultrasound with prostate volume</td><td>Once with retention and a raised creatinine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No hydronephrosis; trabeculated bladder; prostate about 85 mL</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>PSA</td><td>Baseline when it would change management; new baseline about 6 months after starting finasteride, doubled for interpretation</td><td>Up to date</td><td>4.9 ng/mL at baseline before the catheter; PSA density about 0.06</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Digital rectal examination</td><td>Baseline; repeat with clinical change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Done at intake and by urology</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urinalysis and culture</td><td>Baseline; repeat only with symptoms of infection (not for bacteria in catheter urine without symptoms)</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No symptoms of infection</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Catheter care and infection prevention (closed system, bag below the bladder, daily washing, change every four weeks, no routine antibiotics)</td><td>Every visit while catheterized</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Closed system intact; bag below the bladder; meatus clean; no infection</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Tamsulosin effects: dizziness, orthostatic symptoms, falls, nasal stuffiness</td><td>4 to 12 weeks after starting, then every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No dizziness or falls after two weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Urology consultation</td><td>For retention with a raised creatinine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; diagnosis confirmed; finasteride started; trial without catheter planned at about six weeks</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Bladder drained and kidney function back to normal within two weeks. Outcome by 1941-03-03: Creatinine 1.42 mg/dL at presentation, 1.18 mg/dL after two weeks of drainage; no hydronephrosis [S]</item><item>No catheter-associated infection, or prompt treatment if one occurs. In progress; target 1941-03-31 [S]</item><item>A treatment decision that he understands and that fits his values, after a geriatric assessment, by about ten weeks. In progress; target 1941-04-28 [S]</item><item>Voiding without a catheter, with a post-void residual under 300 mL, by about five months. In progress; target 1941-07-14 [S]</item><item>Goals of care and a health care agent recorded. In progress; target 1941-06-09 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia: AUA Guideline, Part I (Initial Work-up and Medical Management) and Part II (Surgical Evaluation and Treatment) (2021); Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH): AUA Guideline Amendment 2023 (2023); AUA White Paper on Nonneurogenic Chronic Urinary Retention: Consensus Definition, Treatment Algorithm, and Outcome End Points (2017); Diagnosis, prevention, and treatment of catheter-associated urinary tract infection in adults: 2009 International Clinical Practice Guidelines from the Infectious Diseases Society of America (2009); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, Benign prostatic hyperplasia monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-week review in four weeks with a trial without catheter. Next scheduled visit 1941-03-31: six-week review with a trial without catheter and a geriatric assessment. [S]</paragraph></text>

